    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13300",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=4m^2+2m-1,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $4m^2+2m-1{:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1 = -2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4{m}^{2}+2m-1 \\\\  & =(4{m}^{2}+2m-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{2{m}^{2}+m-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2m^2+m-1}$ is an integer.",
